### Problems 14 through 17

Problem 14 compares a student-selected line of fit with linear regression. Problem 15 asks whether a sample of 10 randomly selected students supports predictions for other students. A random sample helps, but sample size and population scope still limit the conclusion.

Problem 16 gives `r = 0.946` and asks whether a change in the independent variable causes a change in the dependent variable. A correlation coefficient of 0.946 can occur when a causal relationship exists or when it does not. The correct classification for the source statement is sometimes true. The high correlation alone does not prove causation.

